reviews.review:
An enormous universe that somehow feels tiny
No Man’s Sky has some great ideas. The Corvette is probably my favourite part: building it gradually, flying it, using autopilot, walking around the interior and landing a large ship on planets genuinely feels good.
Unfortunately, most of the game feels like a collection of disconnected systems rather than one coherent universe.
My biggest problem is the lack of scale and discovery. You enter a supposedly unknown system and fleets immediately warp in around you. You land on a new planet and ships are already constantly flying overhead. Almost every system becomes the same pattern: a few planets, a station, markers everywhere and familiar POIs. The galaxy is enormous, but rarely feels remote or mysterious.
The game also seems afraid to challenge the player. Instead of letting scarcity, uncertainty, distance or limitations create satisfaction, it constantly tries to make everything accessible and convenient. Almost everything is marked, teleportation makes distance meaningless, exploration rarely requires real investigation, and even fossils can be freely mixed into imaginary creatures instead of requiring you to reconstruct an actual species.
Then there are endless inconsistencies:
- your suit works in vacuum, but underwater you suddenly need separate oxygen;
- a ship cannot launch without fuel, but can still be magically summoned;
- small refiners need fuel while larger ones do not;
- storage needs electricity while more advanced devices often do not;
- Sentinels instantly switch from ground pursuit to space pursuit when you enter your ship;
- settlements simulate debt and productivity, but becoming profitable mostly rewards you with arbitrary items;
- quest targets repeatedly give intentionally approximate locations just to force the same scanner routine.
There are also only a few dominant alien civilizations, stations quickly become repetitive, and the same small set of planetary structures appears everywhere.
No Man’s Sky has enormous breadth, but very little systemic coherence or meaningful challenge.
The Corvette shows how good the game can be when a system has weight, progression and purpose. I just wish the rest of the universe felt less like Far Cry in space and more like actual exploration.
